1. Always write modular and reusable code following the DRY principle.
2. Maintain consistent naming conventions across files, folders, and variables.
3. Keep the codebase clean, readable, and self-explanatory.
4. Ensure all functions and classes follow the single responsibility principle.
5. Organize code in a structured, predictable, and scalable directory layout.
6. Separate business logic from controllers, views, and services for clarity.
7. Use shared types, interfaces, and constants from centralized modules.
8. Always follow linting and formatting rules (ESLint + Prettier).
9. Remove unused imports, dead code, and commented-out blocks before committing.
10. Enforce strict TypeScript checks and avoid using `any`.
11. Keep configuration and secrets in environment variables, not hardcoded.
12. Use dependency injection and modular patterns for better scalability.
13. Ensure all new code aligns with existing project architecture and patterns.
14. Propose any architectural or design improvements before refactoring.
15. Review all AI-generated code for correctness and consistency.
16. Keep dependencies minimal, stable, and well-maintained.
17. Use consistent commit messages and maintain a changelog for version tracking.
18. Collaborate openly — suggest improvements when better approaches are found.
19. Always handle errors gracefully — ensure proper error messages, logging, and status codes; never leak internal details in responses.
20. Implement proper logging using a centralized logger (e.g., Winston, Pino) with log levels (debug, info, warn, error).
21. Validate all external input (DTOs, query params, request bodies) using class-validator or similar tools to prevent data corruption or injection attacks.
22. Use async/await consistently and avoid unhandled promises.
23. Follow SOLID + Clean Architecture principles — ensure that domain, infrastructure, and presentation layers are clearly separated.
24. Follow repository pattern for all DB operations — keep TypeORM operations isolated from business logic.
25. Encapsulate external service logic (e.g., APIs, queues, storage) in dedicated integration modules to make replacement or scaling easier.
26. For shared libs, ensure proper versioning and backward compatibility to avoid breaking dependent apps.
27. For feature modules, include a dedicated folder structure (controllers, services, repositories, dtos, entities, tests) for clarity and scalability.
28. Sanitize and validate all user input at entry points.
29. Implement proper authorization checks — ensure users can only access allowed data (RBAC/ABAC patterns).
30. Avoid N+1 queries — use relations or joins efficiently in TypeORM.
31. Paginate and filter data in queries instead of fetching large datasets.
32. If an entity includes an `updated_at` or similar timestamp field, ensure a database trigger is created in the migration to automatically update this field whenever the corresponding record is modified.
33. Before implementing any new functionality, thoroughly review the existing codebase to check for missing logic or potential duplication. It’s critical to avoid writing redundant code or re-implementing existing logic.
34. Always follow the existing codebase patterns and conventions to maintain consistency across the project.
35. For TypeORM migrations, strictly use **DDL (Data Definition Language)** queries only — avoid DML (Data Manipulation Language) statements unless absolutely necessary.
36. Do not follow incorrect or non-standard patterns. If you identify code that doesn’t align with industry best practices or established design patterns, highlight it and refactor it end-to-end — including updating associated tests.
37. In the NestJS controller layer, prefer using reusable decorators from the `@shared-utils` library. If you identify opportunities to improve reusability, enhance existing decorators or create new generic ones for current and future use cases. Always ensure controllers remain clean, maintainable, and fully documented in Swagger.
38. When building or refactoring a feature in multiple phases, ensure that the AI (or any assisting system) maintains a detailed internal reference of all previous design decisions, implemented modules, and pending tasks. This ensures continuity, avoids rework, and helps the AI stay contextually aware throughout the entire development cycle.
39. **ALWAYS check `AI_FEEDBACK.md` before implementing any new entities, DTOs, or modules.** This file contains documented mistakes and correct patterns to follow. Reference it to avoid repeating past errors and ensure consistency with established coding patterns.
